.TH RECYCLE 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
recycle \- move files and directories to the Recycle Bin
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B recycle
.RI [ path ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B recycle
sends each
.IR path ,
a file or a directory with all its contents, to the Recycle Bin, without
asking for confirmation and without showing progress.
It is faster than
.B rm \-rf
and reversible: restore items from the Recycle Bin in Explorer.
.PP
A relative
.I path
is resolved against the current directory.
Each
.I path
is handled separately, and a failure does not stop the rest.
With no
.I path
operands,
.B recycle
does nothing.
.SH OPTIONS
None.
Every argument is a path, even one that begins with a dash, and
.B \-\-
is not special.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every
.I path
was recycled.
.TP
.B 1
At least one
.I path
could not be recycled.
.PP
Each failure is reported on standard error as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
recycle: \fIpath\fR: \fImessage\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
where
.I message
is the Windows error message, or
.B Could not recycle
for errors specific to the shell's file operations.
.SH EXAMPLES
Recycle a build directory and some object files:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ recycle build *.o
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
.B recycle
uses the Windows shell's delete operation with undo.
Where the Recycle Bin is unavailable, such as on a network share or a USB
flash drive, or for items too large for it, Windows deletes the items
permanently, without warning.
.PP
Windows treats
.B *
and
.B ?\&
in the last component of a
.I path
as wildcards.
.PP
Error messages are written only when standard error is a console.
.PP
Storage Sense can delete old Recycle Bin items automatically, which
pairs well with
.BR recycle .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rm (1),
.BR rmdir (1)
